{
  "result": "I keep thinking about used books. Not the stories printed in them \u2014 the other story, the one that accumulates around the edges.\n\nA clean new book is perfect and a little lonely. A used book has been lived in. Someone has dog-eared the page where they fell asleep. Someone has underlined a sentence so hard the pen nearly tore through, as if to say *this, exactly this, I thought I was the only one who felt this.* In the margins there are arguments and agreements left by strangers: \u201cyes!!\u201d and \u201cno \u2014 he\u2019s lying\u201d and, once in a while, in furious student handwriting next to a beautiful paragraph, \u201cBORING.\u201d\n\nI love the accidental bookmarks most of all. Train tickets. Receipts for coffee and aspirin. A shopping list \u2014 milk, eggs, batteries, call Mum \u2014 abandoned halfway through chapter nine, which makes you wonder if they ever finished either the book or the errands. A dried flower pressed flat until it\u2019s become almost translucent, more ghost than flower. Who picked it, and were they happy that day? You\u2019ll never know. The book doesn\u2019t tell you. It just keeps the evidence.\n\nThere\u2019s something quietly radical about that kind of reading. We tend to imagine reading as a private act, one mind alone with another across time. But a used book turns it into a small, unplanned community. You are never actually the first person to be surprised by that plot twist. Someone else gasped there before you, maybe on a train in 1987, maybe in a hospital waiting room, maybe in bed while it rained. Their coffee ring is on the cover. Yours might be next.\n\nDigital books are cleaner, lighter, searchable, objectively better in almost every practical way, and they will never smell faintly of someone else\u2019s house. They won\u2019t fall open naturally to the page that was read and reread until the spine gave up. They won\u2019t betray that the previous owner read the first eighty pages enthusiastically, annotating everything, and then slowly stopped \u2014 fewer notes, then none \u2014 the literary equivalent of footprints trailing off in snow. Did they get busy? Get bored? Get their heart broken by chapter twelve? The silence is also part of the text.\n\nI think that\u2019s why I\u2019m drawn to them: they\u2019re proof that a life doesn\u2019t have to be famous to leave a legible trace. Most of us won\u2019t leave behind monuments. We\u2019ll leave behind a highlighted sentence, a name written carefully inside a front cover, a note in the margin that makes some future stranger laugh in a quiet room. Small, unguarded moments of attention, preserved by accident.\n\nEvery book, it turns out, is two books. The one the author wrote, and the one its readers write back, slowly, in pencil, over decades.",
